# GitHub Audit Log Shipping to XSIAM

## Purpose

GitHub Enterprise audit logs are retained in Amazon S3 and sent to Cortex XSIAM for security monitoring. This runbook explains how the integration works, how to check it, and how to restore the data flow.

XSIAM is the MoJ's chosen Security Operations Platform. All major platforms are required to enable log ingestion into it, including GitHub.

The infrastructure is managed in the [`github-audit-log-stream` Terraform component](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform-environments/tree/main/terraform/environments/developer-experience/github-audit-log-stream). The implementation is tracked in [engineering platform issue 76](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/ministry-of-justice-engineering-platform/issues/76).

## Audience

This runbook is for Developer Experience engineers who maintain the AWS and GitHub configuration. The [Monitoring and Integration Platform (MIP) team](https://justiceuk.sharepoint.com/sites/knowthething/SitePages/MonitoringandResponse.aspx) owns the Cortex XSIAM collector.

You need the appropriate GitHub Enterprise Owner and Modernisation Platform production account access to complete all of the checks in this runbook.

## Architecture

The production data flow is:

1. GitHub Enterprise streams audit log objects to the `mojdevx-production-github-audit-log-stream` S3 bucket using GitHub's OpenID Connect (OIDC) identity provider.
2. An `s3:ObjectCreated` event sends a message to the `github-audit-log-stream-cortex-xsiam` Amazon Simple Queue Service (SQS) queue.
3. Cortex XSIAM uses workload identity federation to assume the `github-audit-log-stream-cortex-xsiam` AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) role.
4. Cortex reads the SQS message, retrieves and decrypts the referenced S3 object, then deletes the processed message.
5. A message moves to the encrypted dead-letter queue after five unsuccessful receives. Messages in both queues are retained for 14 days.

S3 objects are encrypted with an AWS Key Management Service (KMS) customer-managed key and versioning is enabled. Objects move to Standard-Infrequent Access after 90 days, move to Glacier Instant Retrieval after 395 days, and are deleted after 730 days. The same lifecycle applies to non-current versions.

This integration covers audit events from all organisations in the `ministry-of-justice-uk` GitHub Enterprise account.

## Monitor the Integration

The MIP team has an alert when GitHub audit log data has not reached Cortex XSIAM for more than three hours.

Use AWS CloudWatch metrics to check each stage of the integration:

* Confirm that new objects are arriving in the S3 bucket.
* Confirm that the main SQS queue's `NumberOfMessagesSent`, `NumberOfMessagesReceived`, and `NumberOfMessagesDeleted` metrics increase as new audit logs arrive.
* Check `ApproximateNumberOfMessagesVisible` for a growing backlog.
* Check `ApproximateNumberOfMessagesNotVisible` for messages that are repeatedly being received but not deleted.
* Confirm that the dead-letter queue's `ApproximateNumberOfMessagesVisible` remains at zero.

`NumberOfEmptyReceives` can increase after Cortex drains the queue. This is expected and does not indicate a fault by itself.

## Investigate an Alert or Missing Logs

Check the delivery path in order so that you can identify which team owns the fault.

### 1. Check GitHub to S3 Delivery

1. Open the production S3 bucket and check the timestamp of the latest audit log object.
2. If no recent objects exist, ask a GitHub Enterprise Owner to check the audit log streaming status in the [`ministry-of-justice-uk` audit log stream settings](https://github.com/enterprises/ministry-of-justice-uk/settings/audit-log/streams).
3. Confirm that the configured bucket and IAM role match the Terraform-managed production resources.
4. Review recent Terraform changes and failed deployment workflows for the `github-audit-log-stream` component.

Do not reconfigure or remove the GitHub stream until you have captured its current settings and checked for an active incident.

### 2. Check S3 to SQS Delivery

If S3 objects are current but `NumberOfMessagesSent` is not increasing:

1. Check that the bucket has an `s3:ObjectCreated:*` notification for the Cortex XSIAM queue.
2. Check the SQS queue policy allows the S3 service to send messages from the audit log bucket and production AWS account.
3. Check the KMS key policy allows S3 to generate data keys and decrypt queue notifications.
4. Restore missing configuration through Terraform. Do not make a permanent manual infrastructure change in the AWS console.

### 3. Check XSIAM Consumption

If messages are visible on the main queue but are not being received or deleted:

1. Check whether the MIP team has reported a Cortex collector incident or configuration change.
2. Confirm that the `github-audit-log-stream/cortex-xsiam-workload-identity` secret exists in AWS Secrets Manager and contains non-placeholder `issuer_url`, `audience`, and `service_account` values. Do not share these values in Slack, tickets, or logs.
3. Confirm that the Cortex IAM role trust policy uses the expected Google federated principal, audience, and service account subject.
4. Confirm that the role can receive and delete SQS messages, list the bucket, retrieve objects, and decrypt them with KMS.
5. Contact the MIP team using the [Monitoring and Response page](https://justiceuk.sharepoint.com/sites/knowthething/SitePages/MonitoringandResponse.aspx) and provide the incident time, queue URL, IAM role ARN, and relevant metric behaviour. Do not send secret values.

### 4. Check the Dead-letter Queue

If the dead-letter queue contains messages:

1. Record when the count started increasing and correlate it with the main queue and S3 metrics.
2. Ask the MIP team to check the Cortex collector errors for the same period.
3. Resolve the identity, permission, decryption, or object-processing error before redriving messages.
4. Redrive the messages to the main queue and confirm that `NumberOfMessagesDeleted` increases and both queue backlogs return to zero.

Do not delete messages from either queue as a troubleshooting step. Queue messages expire after 14 days, so escalate promptly when the dead-letter queue is not empty.

## Update the Cortex Workload Identity

The workload identity values are stored in the production Secrets Manager secret named `github-audit-log-stream/cortex-xsiam-workload-identity`. Terraform creates the secret but deliberately ignores changes to its value.

When MIP changes the Cortex identity:

1. Obtain the new issuer URL, audience, and service account identifier from the MIP team through an approved secure channel.
2. Update the existing secret value, preserving the JSON keys `issuer_url`, `audience`, and `service_account`.
3. Run the [Terraform plan and apply workflow](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform-environments/actions/workflows/developer-experience.yml) for the production `github-audit-log-stream` component. The identity values determine whether the Cortex resources and trust policy are created.
4. Give MIP the resulting Cortex IAM role ARN and SQS queue URL through an approved channel.
5. Ask MIP to test the connection and confirm that new messages are received and deleted.
6. Check that no messages remain in the dead-letter queue.

## Change the Infrastructure

Make all infrastructure changes in the [`modernisation-platform-environments`](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform-environments/tree/main/terraform/environments/developer-experience/github-audit-log-stream) repository and use its pull request plan and deployment process. Keep the following controls in place:

* least-privilege permissions for GitHub, S3, SQS, KMS, and Cortex
* encryption for the bucket, queues, dead-letter queue, secret, and Terraform state
* S3 versioning and the documented 730-day retention policy
* workload identity conditions for both the audience and service account subject
* a redrive policy for messages Cortex cannot process

After deployment, generate a GitHub audit event and confirm the new S3 object, SQS message delivery, Cortex receipt and deletion, and an empty dead-letter queue.
